r grass and silage"\n            *   "In the case of sulfuric acid-catalyzed hydrothermal pretreatment..."\n            *   "The heating process was the same as that for the organosolv pretreatment..."\n            *   "The use of H2SO4 as a catalyst in the hydrothermal pretreatment..."\n        *   **Comparison/Controls:** Mentions "non-catalyzed organosolv and hydrothermal pretreatments (auto-catalyzed organosolv and auto-catalyzed hydrothermal) of Napier grass and silage were also used as controls."\n        *   **Scanning for Oxidizers:** I am looking for keywords like "oxygen", "oxidizer", "photocatalysis", "TiO2", "light", "UV", "bromine", "chlorine", etc.\n        *   **Results Discussion (3.1):** Mentions mechanism of NaOH, H2SO4, auto-catalysis. No mention of TiO2.\n        *   **Results Discussion (3.2):** Mentions pseudo-lignin, SEM.\n        *   **Methods:** I need to check specifically for any use of oxidative agents like NaOH (alkali), H2SO4 (acid), or any oxidizer.\n        *   **Search terms in Methods:** "oxidation", "oxidizer", "TiO2", "photocatalysis", "UV".\n        *   **Findings:** The methodology describes alkali (NaOH) and acid (H2SO4) catalysts used in the organosolv and hydrothermal processes. It does not mention any oxidative agent such as TiO2 photocatalysis. NaOH is not typically classified as an "oxidizer agent" in the sense of an oxidative pretreatment method (like ozonation or photocatalytic oxidation), although it acts as an alkali. H2SO4 is an acid. Neither fits the definition of an "oxidizer agent" in the context of oxidative pretreatment.\n\n3.  **Evaluate Against the Question:**\n    *   Does the methodology explicitly describe the use of an **oxidative pretreatment by the use of an oxidizer agent**?\n    *   **NaOH/H2SO4:** These are catalysts. The text does not label them as "oxidizers" in a pretreatment context (e.g., ozonation, photocatalysis). Even alkali pretreatment isn\'t always termed an oxidative pretreatment unless spe