ess... A 20-filter paper unit (FPU) cellulase/g-biomass... were added to each bottle."\n-   "A 20-filter paper unit (FPU) cellulase/g-biomass...".\n\nThe question is specifically about the *pretreatment* in the context of AD (Anaerobic Digestion), which implies *pre-treatment of the substrate* before *incorporation in the digestion*.\nThe methodologies describe:\n1.  Organosolv pretreatment (using NaOH or H2SO4).\n2.  Hydrothermal pretreatment (using H2SO4).\n\nThere is a mention of "cellulase/g-biomass" in Section 2.4 "SSF and two-stage hydrogen and methane production".\nThe prompt asks: "Does the methodology explicitly describe the use of an enzyme for pretreatment on a substrate (this also includes crude enzyme treatments), This exclude any bacteria, fungi, or other secreting enzyme organism, it refers to the direct use of enzymes. Before answering: 1. Identify enzyme used in the methodology 2.ensure it is an enzyme preparation or extracted crude enzyme 3.ensure that the enzymes are used directly and not produced in situ by bacteria, fungi, or other enzyme-secreting organisms 4. answer false if any requirement are not satisfied"\n\nThe question is slightly confused in the preamble ("Pretreatment should be understood as..."). I need to answer True or False based on whether the methodology describes the use of enzymes for pretreatment.\nLooking at Section 2.3, pretreatment is described as:\n-   Sodium hydroxide as catalyst for organosolv.\n-   Sulfuric acid as catalyst for organosolv.\n-   Sulfuric acid as catalyst for hydrothermal.\n\nLooking at Section 2.4, there is mention of "cellulase/g-biomass".\nCellulase is an enzyme.\nHowever, does the *pretreatment* section use an enzyme?\nThe text in Section 2.3 says: "For the organosolv pretreatment, sodium hydroxide was applied as a catalyst... while sulfuric acid was used as a catalyst...". "For the hydrothermal pretreatment, sulfuric acid was used as the catalyst...".\nIn Section 2.4 (SSF): "A 20-filter paper unit (F