2026-05-27 22:03:25,452 ['[false]', 'The user wants to know if the methodology section of the provided article explicitly describes using a microbial pretreatment on a substrate (like bacterial cultures or microbia consortia) for the context of anaerobic digestion.\nThe question specifies to exclude mediums rich in microorganisms (digestate, sludge) and fungi, extracted enzymes, etc.\nI need to look only at the "2 Material and methods" section of the article.\nThe question asks if pretreatment was applied to the substrate (i.e., the whiskey byproducts mixture) using microbial treatments (microbes added/used to treat the substrate, or microorganisms involved in the pretreatment step itself acting on the substrate).\nWait, the question is asking if pretreatment involved a microbial pretreatment on a substrate like isolate bacterial cultures or specialised microbia consortia. This means did the method describe adding microbes or using them to treat the whiskey byproducts *before* digestion (pre-treatment phase)?\nThe text says "Pretreatment group without the addition of H_2SO_4... was set to investigate...".\nIt lists methods:\n2.2 Pretreatment of whiskey byproducts\n2.3 Biomethane potential assays\n2.4 Analytical methods\n2.5 Microbial analysis\n2.5 says "After the BMP assays, liquid samples were taken from all the digesters for microbial analysis...".\nIt does not describe using bacteria or fungi to treat the substrate itself during the digestion process *as a pretreatment* to the substrate.\nIt mentions "The inoculum... was sourced from...".\nPretreatment methods:\n- Untreated group\n- Steam-treated group (autoclave at 135 °C for 15 min)\n- Dilute H2SO4 treated group\n- NaOH solution adjusted pH to 6.5–7.0 for methanogenesis.\n\nThis is chemical/thermal pretreatment, not microbial pretreatment on the substrate.\nThe inoculum is added *after* pretreatment (implied), but the substrate itself (whiskey byproducts) was not treated by microbes before adding to the digeste