ditions, or typical scientific knowledge. There is no need for complex reasoning."\nThe instruction also says: "Avoid false positives by overinterpretation: pay attention to the entire question, mention are not sufficient they should satisfy all the requirement from the questions."\nThe question: "Does the methodology explicitly describe any physical process that mechanically alters the substrate..."\nThe methodology section 2.1 explicitly says: "We applied three successive mechanical pretreatments... shredding... II-PT and III-PT... specifications... II-PT and III-PT were carried out with water addition...".\n2.1 says: "shredding (I-PT code in Table 1)... whose role is to reduce the particle size".\nThis is explicit. It is not just equipment description, but it links equipment to a function (size reduction).\nIt explicitly states the substrate is the feedstock.\nThe methodology does not apply these to inoculum or during analysis.\nSo, True.\n\nOne more check: "Do NOT infer pretreatment type from equipment, conditions, or typical scientific knowledge." - The text clearly states the *role* of the equipment (e.g. "reduce particle size", "alter the lignocellulosic structure"). This is explicitly written, not inferred from "wet grinders" as an idiom.\nThe text explicitly states: "mechanical pretreatments are the most applied operations... There is a wide range of mechanical technologies... hammer and knife mill being the most used...".\nSection 2.1 details the pretreatment functions.\n\nOkay, confident in True.\n\nWait, one nuance: The question asks "Does the methodology explicitly describe any physical process that mechanically alters the substrate...?"\nThe text says: "We applied three successive mechanical pretreatments... We applied these three operations sequentially."\nThen under 2.1: "I-PT code... role is to reduce the particle size".\n"II-PT... role is to favor the extraction of organic compounds...".\n"III-PT... role is to alter the lignocellulosic structure...